ate change and  that has been reflected in the poll as  well  more than 80 of people for example said  that they understood what we mean by  renewable energy so that's things like  wind and solar  more than 80 of people again also said  that they understand what we mean by  carbon footprint that's the amount of  emissions we all create in our own daily  lives however there wasn't a huge amount  of understanding of the term cop 26  now that's the landmark climate  conference which is going to be held  by the uk in glasgow at the end of the  year and we're definitely going to be  hearing a lot more about that  we are lisa thank you and if you're  unclear on any of those terms like net  zero or cop 26 just head to our website  skynews.com  climate for our a to z glossary  now a snap election in greenland has  exposed tensions between politics  business  mining and renewable technologies  today's result  a win by a left-wing environmentalist  party puts the future of big rare earth  mining projects there in doubt  now rare earth minerals are crucial to  reach emissions targets they're used in  everything from wind turbines to  electric cars  and even combat aircraft so here's where  they're found and the highest number of  reserves are in china with approximately  44  million tonnes second is vietnam  followed by brazil  but today all eyes were on greenland  where companies want to exploit the one  and a half million tonnes of  untapped minerals now the result of the  election casts  doubt on the mining complex here in the  south of the island which many  greenlanders oppose  because the minerals are notoriously  dirty to process  so here's all you need to know about  rare earth elements in 60 seconds  [Music]  so  [Music]  [Music]  our defense and security correspondent  alistair bunkel joins me now and  alastair it's not just greenland where  there are potential tensions over these  very precious resources is it  no it's not and it's not just mobile  phones electric cars perhaps in the  television that you're watching this on  that has been built using rare earth  minerals but  also vital military technology like the  f-35  fifth generation stealth fighter jets  like new submarines and  anti-missile devices and that is why  rare earth minerals have been  nicknamed industrial gold and it's why  they're so  vital to national security that the  recent british  integrated foreign policy and security  review  put them down as a global security issue  in the years to come why well as you  outlined because of  china china is dominant in the mining  and the production  of railroad rare earth minerals so much  so  that if there was a wider trade war  we've seen some of that between the u.s  and china already  or if there was a conflict between china  and the west then they could dominate  and cut off the supply line so that is  why britain  and others have been watching this  election of only 56 000 people  so closely alistair thank you  let's take a look now at some of the  day's other climate news and a big  increase in the number of  new coal-fired power stations in china  last year  cancelled out all the other closures  around the rest of the world  global energy monitor found that china  commissioned 38.4 gigawatts of new coal  plants in 2020  more than three-quarters of the global  total  several people have been arrested after  extinction rebellion activists broke  windows at the london headquarters of  barclays bank  the protesters say they want to expose  the banking system and its investment in  fossil fuels  berkeley says they're aiming to be a net  zero bank by 2050  and their financing portfolio is aligned  to the goals of the paris agreement  so-called chelsea tractors are living up  to their reputation  as a go-to choice for city drivers  75 of all suvs sold in the uk are  registered to people  living in towns and cities even though  they produce  more carbon emissions than other new  petrol cars  the british army is launc